2. Connection Architecture in JDeveloper 14c
  ===========================================
  
  JDeveloper 14c decouples database storage from application code using a hierarchical metadata layered approach.
  
  [Oracle Database Server] <--- (JDBC thin Driver) <--- [JDeveloper Data Connection]
                                                               |
                                                   [ADF Model Binding Layer]
                                                               |
                                                   [ADF Business Components]

  Step-by-Step Configuration inside JDeveloper
  ---------------------------------------------
  1. Open the Window: Navigate to View -> Resources (or Databases window).
  2. Launch the Connection Wizard: Expand the IDE Connections tree, right-click on Database, and click New Database Connection.
  3. Fill Out Connection Properties:
        1. Connection Name: HrConn (Keep it a valid Java identifier).
        2. Connection Type: Oracle (JDBC).
        3. Username / Password: Provide credentials for your dedicated schema.
        4. Driver: thin.
        5. Host Name: localhost (or your targeted database server IP).
        6. JDBC Port: 1521 (Standard Oracle listener port).
        7. Service Name: Provide your specific pluggable database path (e.g., ORCLPDB1 or freepdb1).

          Test Connection: Click Test Connection. Once it displays Success, click OK.
            
  Modern 14c Specific Option: Oracle Cloud DB WalletIf we need an Autonomous Database environment, 
  JDeveloper 14c includes a native connection option named "Oracle Cloud DB using Wallet". 
    This avoids needing a hardcoded IP and port by natively consuming your secure cloud infrastructure zip file credential package.

 3. Exposing the Connection to the ADF Model Layer
    =============================================
    Once your IDE Connection works, it needs to be attached to your application project environment so the workspace components can read the underlying tables:
    When executing the Day 1 task of creating Business Components, selecting HrConn as the base scheme automatically writes structural mapping configuration arrays inside your project's local bc4j.xcfg file.

This setup makes sure that when your application is finally built and deployed onto a running WebLogic Server 14c Instance, it translates dynamically into an enterprise Application JDBC Data Source.
